/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A screw thread has three diameters.
   - M20 × 2.5 means a 20 mm major diameter and a 2.5 mm pitch (plain M20 means the coarse pitch).
   - A bolt in tension breaks across the threaded part, so its stress is taken on the tensile stress area:
   - As is in mm², d is the major diameter in mm and p the pitch in mm.
   - A bolt in shear is checked on the shank area πd²/4, or on the core area when threads cross the shear plane.
   - A bolt is tightened before the service load arrives.
   - When an external tensile load P is applied, the bolt stretches a little more and the clamped plates relax a little.
   - C = kb/(kb + kc), where kb is the bolt stiffness and kc the stiffness of the clamped parts.
   - When the load lies in the plane of the joint, every bolt is in shear.
   - A bracket bolted to a column face, with a load P hanging at distance e from the face, tries to tilt about its bottom edge.
*/
