/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A butt weld joins plates edge to edge and fills the full thickness (single-V, double-V, U or J edge preparation).
   - A fillet weld joins two surfaces at a right angle and has a triangular section with leg (size) h.
   - P is the load one fillet weld carries in N; h the leg in mm; L the effective length in mm (actual length minus about 2h for the craters at start and stop); τ the permissible shear stress in MPa.
   - Parallel fillet welds are always checked in shear on the throat.
   - When the load lies in the plane of the weld group but misses its centroid G, move it to G and add a torque T = P·e.
   - A bracket welded to the face of a column, with the load P at distance e from the face, puts the welds in bending about a horizontal axis through G.
   - A is the throat area in mm²; I the second moment of the throat areas about the bending axis in mm⁴ (two vertical welds of length L: I = 2 × 0.707h·L³/12); y the distance from that axis to the weld end in mm.
   - The two stresses act at right angles on the throat, so they are combined as a resultant τ = √(σb² + τ₁²), which is the convention of Shigley and Bhandari.
   - The permissible shear stress comes from the electrode, the code and the inspection level, and examiners state it.
*/
